  <name><text>Ch03_P03_Ex5_Product_of_irrationals</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Conjecture: the product of two different irrational numbers is always irrational.</p>
<p>Give a counterexample. Type \(\sqrt{2}\) as sqrt(2) and \(\pi\) as pi.</p>
<div>First number: [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>
<div>Second number: [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>A counterexample needs two numbers that satisfy the conditions of the conjecture (both irrational, and different from each other) whose product is rational.</p>
<p>Take \(\sqrt{2}\) and \(\sqrt{8}=2\sqrt{2}\). Both are irrational: \(\sqrt{2}\) is irrational, and if \(2\sqrt{2}=\dfrac{p}{q}\) with \(p\), \(q\) integers, then \(\sqrt{2}=\dfrac{p}{2q}\) would be rational. They are different. Their product is \(\sqrt{2}\times\sqrt{8}=\sqrt{16}=4\), which is rational. So the conjecture is false. (The irrationality of \(\sqrt{2}\) is proved in a later chapter, by contradiction.)</p>
<p>Other counterexamples: \(\sqrt{2}\) and \(-\sqrt{2}\) (product \(-2\)); \(\sqrt{2}\) and \(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\) (product \(1\)); \(1+\sqrt{2}\) and \(1-\sqrt{2}\) (product \(-1\)); \(\pi\) and \(\dfrac{1}{\pi}\) (product \(1\)).</p>
<p>Some choices do not count. \(0\) is rational (\(0=\dfrac{0}{1}\)), so \(0\times\sqrt{2}=0\) is not a counterexample. \(\sqrt{4}=2\) is rational. And \(\sqrt{2}\times\sqrt{2}=2\) uses the same number twice, but the conjecture is about two <em>different</em> irrational numbers.</p>
<p>A true statement nearby: the product of a non-zero rational number and an irrational number is irrational.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
